/// Chapter 46 of the guide, written from md-docs/46-upgrading.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page UpgradingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/upgrading", title: "Upgrading", description: "Installing a new version, what changed in 5.10, 5.9, 5.6, 5.0, 4.2, 4.1, 4.0.1 and 4.0, and moving a WebFluent 2 or 3 project forward with wf migrate.", layout: DocsShell("Upgrading", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "46" }), blurb: "What changed in each release, what to do when you upgrade, and how wf migrate carries an older project forward.", source: "md-docs/46-upgrading.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "installing-a-newer-version", label: "Installing a newer version"), Anchor(id: "5-10", label: "5.10"), Anchor(id: "5-9", label: "5.9"), Anchor(id: "5-6", label: "5.6"), Anchor(id: "5-0", label: "5.0"), Anchor(id: "4-2", label: "4.2"), Anchor(id: "4-1", label: "4.1"), Anchor(id: "4-0", label: "4.0"), Anchor(id: "3-x", label: "3.x"), Anchor(id: "from-webfluent-2", label: "From WebFluent 2")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/troubleshooting", prevLabel: "45. {t("ch.45")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/browser-support", nextLabel: "47. {t("ch.47")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Heading("Installing a newer version", id: "installing-a-newer-version").h2

        Markdown("Run the installer again — it replaces the binary in place — or `cargo install webfluent --force`. `wf --version` says what you have. Update `wf-lsp` and the editor extension with it: the language server is built from the same compiler.\n\nThe full notes for every release are in [`RELEASE_NOTES.md`](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/blob/master/RELEASE_NOTES.md); what follows is what an upgrade asks of you.")

        Heading("5.10", id: "5-10").h2

        Markdown("5.10 makes pages, pre-renders and tests do what the guide says, and a project may notice these:\n\n- **Column spans leave room for the gap.** A `Column` in a `Row` with a gap is narrower by its share of the gap, and spans that add to 12 sit on one line instead of wrapping the last. A layout that made up for it, `span: 8` beside `span: 3`, now leaves a space; give it back its `4`. The spans read the gap from `--wf-gap`, which `gap:` sets, so a gap your own CSS gives a `Row` (`style \{ gap: 4px \}`) needs `--wf-gap: 4px` beside it. A PDF now lays a `Column` out at its `span:`, and a `calc()` of a percentage and points is read against the box it lands in, as a browser does.\n- **A `Menu`'s `label:` names its list, not its button.** The button says what `trigger:` says. `Menu(label: \"File\")` with neither `trigger:` nor `visible:` now draws a button that says *Menu*; write `trigger: \"File\"`. A menu that `visible:` opens no longer draws a button. A `Dropdown`'s `label:` is still its button.\n- **A `Form` without `bind:` asks the `validate` rules** of its page or component before `on submit`, and shows their messages instead of submitting while one fails. Each copy of a component with `Form(bind: …)` has its own form, where all its copies shared one.\n- **A bound `Checkbox` or `Radio` with its own `on change` writes its state first.** The binding's write used to be lost, so a handler that wrote the state itself now writes it a second time, and one that toggles it (`agreed = !agreed`) undoes the click: take that write out. A `Switch`'s `on change`, which never ran, runs.\n- **Text that reads state follows it.** Control labels, a `Table`'s `caption:`, tab titles, tooltip text, a modal's `title:`, a menu's trigger, an `Avatar`, a `Skeleton`'s sizes and an `Icon`'s positional name kept the value they were drawn with; they now change with it. A `Checkbox(label: t(\"…\"))` changes language with `setLocale`.\n- **`setLocale` is remembered.** The choice is kept in the reader's `localStorage` under `wf:locale`, and the next page opens in it; a `?lang=` address still wins. A `persist` store kept for the same purpose still works beside it.\n- **The `Navbar` takes the theme's surface and text colours**, so a dark or custom theme no longer gets a white bar.\n- **The Escape that closes an open `Modal` or `Dialog` stays there**: a page's `on key(\"Escape\")` no longer runs for it.\n- **An anchored layer hides with its anchor** when the anchor scrolls out of view, and stays open; style it through `[data-anchor-hidden]` if needed.\n- **A `Splitter`'s `reset:` is written as given**, not clamped to `min:` and `max:`, and the first drag or key press moves from the edge as drawn.\n- **A keyed list slides only the items whose index changes.** One that keeps its index while its place on screen moves now jumps there.\n- **An `Avatar` whose `src:` is a run-time `data:` or `blob:` address** gets an empty `src`, as any other `src:` does.\n- **A site that frames its own page**, with a `Frame` whose `src:` is a site-relative string (`\"/preview/\"`, `\"/embed/\{id\}\"`), is served `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` and `frame-ancestors 'self'`. One whose same-site address comes from a variable is still served `DENY`, so the browser still refuses it; write the address as a string, such as `\"/embed/\{id\}\"`.\n- **Stores**: a store's derived values keep following their state after the first page that read the store has gone, and one that reads a `derived` written below it follows it. The built script declares a store after the stores it `use`s.\n- **Pre-rendered HTML matches the live page.** A `Menu` or `Dropdown` is its button and a hidden list, so its entries no longer show before the script runs, and a menu that state opens is its list alone; a `Checkbox`, `Radio` or `Switch` has its `<input>`, with the control's `id:` on it; a part written with flags carries their classes; a `Column` with a literal `span:` carries `wf-col--N`; `disabled`, `required` and `controls` are left off when false, or when the build cannot know them.\n- **`wf test` snapshots change** where they hold a `Menu`, a `Dropdown`, a `Column` with a `span:`, a `keyLabel`, a count in a locale with more plural forms than English, or a `disabled:`, `required:` or `controls:` that is false: look at the difference and run `wf test --update`. An `expect` for a `Menu`'s `label:` text no longer finds it (it is an `aria-label`), and one for its `trigger:` does. `expect` reads `&#x27;` as an apostrophe, and in a test that acts compares with white space collapsed. A `click` may now find a tab, a menu item or a radio button it used to miss.\n- **Fewer warnings and errors**: an `Icon` naming one of the 23 new icons draws it instead of warning `V08`; `on auxclick`, `on focusin` and `on focusout` no longer warn `V09`; `on blur` and `on focus` outside an element compile, where they were an error; and `() => value` fits a function of no arguments, where it was `T01`.\n- **Markdown reads backslash escapes.** A text with `\\*`, `\\_`, `\\#`, `\\[` and the like now shows the character alone, and it takes no part in emphasis or a link; `\\\\` is one backslash. A backslash at the end of a paragraph's last line is shown, where it was dropped. A link or picture whose address has a scheme a page refuses (`javascript:`, `data:`) is drawn as its words.\n- **Built-ins write what they are given.** `role:`, `tabindex:`, `title:`, `hidden:`, `aria-*`, `data-*` and `on` handlers on a `VirtualList`, `Splitter`, `Tabs`, `Sidebar`, `Breadcrumb`, `Slider`, `DatePicker`, `FileUpload`, the layers and the rest now reach their element, where some were dropped; `label:` on a `VirtualList`, `Splitter` or `Carousel` is painted as its `aria-label`, not as text in it; `disabled: true` on a `Slider`, `DatePicker` or `FileUpload` disables it; a `Sidebar`'s `id:` is its id. Pre-rendered HTML and `wf test` snapshots carry these too: look at the difference and run `wf test --update`.\n- **A modal's Escape and title.** Over an open popover, the first Escape closes the modal and the next the popover; a press inside the modal no longer closes the popover beneath. Pre-rendered, a modal's title is its heading. A tooltip under the pointer lets Escape go on to the page.\n- **Tests read as a reader does.** An `expect` that counted on a space between two elements that touch (\"live : …\") now reads them touching. A test run in a browser has the project's own CSS, so CSS that hides or changes text changes what it sees, and a stylesheet naming a file not in `public/` fails it. Snapshots lose `data-*` that were `false` and gain a call's `data-*`, `aria-*`, `id` and `class:` on a component's root: run `wf test --update` after looking at the difference.\n- **`data-x: true` passed through a component is `\"true\"`**, where it was `\"\"`: a selector on `[data-x=\"\"]` stops matching.\n- **A local of a member's name is the action's own.** A store action that wrote a member through a `let` of the same name now writes the local.\n- **`Portal` is a built-in's name**: a project's own component or store called `Portal` is reported.\n- **On Windows, type `webfluent`** (or `wf.exe`) wherever the guide says `wf`. Each earlier `.msi` had an `UpgradeCode` of its own, so the new one cannot replace it: uninstall the old one from *Apps*, or both stay installed and on `PATH`.\n- **A tool built on the compiler** adds the new fields where it writes a struct literal: `doc` on `StateDecl`, `DerivedDecl` and `ActionDecl`, `frames_itself` on `BuildConfig`, `only_route` on `BuildOptions` and `pages_deferred` on `BuildReport`. It adds a `Length::Mix(..)` arm where it matches `paged::style::Length`, and passes a locale to `testing::render_test` as its fourth argument. `print::statement` text for a documented member starts at its `///`, so replace from `print::written_start`. `check_project_via` starts threads, and calls a `Project`'s `file_of` and `source_of` up front.\n\nRun `wf check` after upgrading.")

        Heading("5.9", id: "5-9").h2

        Markdown("5.9 holds a few things to what the guide already said, and a project may notice them:\n\n- **A comma between two style declarations is an error** (`E002`). `style \{ width: \{w\}px, flex: none \}` compiled to one declaration whose value was the rest of the line, so the browser dropped both; write each on its own line, or separate them with `;`. A comma inside a value (`font-family: a, b`) is untouched.\n- **A store's `persist … key:` is kept.** It was dropped, so the value was kept under no key; now it follows the key as a page's does. What is already stored is found under the same names.\n- **A refined type's bounds are checked as the reader types** where a `validate` block guards the state: `Number(0..10)` no longer takes 10, and `Date(after: …)` and `before:` are held. A field that accepted its open end now says *Too large*.\n- **`Card(animate: .Drop)` plays a project's own animation** on an element that stands still. It did nothing before, so a page may now move where it did not.\n- **A count of exactly 0 shows `key.zero`** wherever the translation file has one, in any language, and the pre-render picks each count's form by the default locale's rules rather than English's. A `zero` message that never showed now does.\n- **A site whose default locale is right to left** is written `<html lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">` from the first paint; `meta.lang` names a region of the default locale's language, and is otherwise the default locale.\n- **New warnings:** `A17` (a `Frame` with no title) and `V12` (a long list that is not windowed). `wf check` names each with its fix.")

        Heading("5.6", id: "5-6").h2

        Markdown("5.6 refuses a name the language already has as a program's own ([`E118`](/docs/guide/diagnostics#e118-a-name-the-language-already-has)): a `state env`, a loop's `t`, a `persist theme`, an action named `update`. Each compiled before and read the language's value where the program meant its own — a page's `state env` showed `[object Object]`. Run `wf check`: the message names the declaration and suggests a name of your own, and renaming it and its reads is the whole change.")

        Heading("5.0", id: "5-0").h2

        Markdown("5.0 makes the compiler refuse much more of what used to compile and then fail in the browser. A project that built under 4.x may stop on errors it always had. Start with a check, which writes nothing:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf check")
            ])

        Markdown("Every finding names its code, its place and what to do; `wf explain CODE` prints the code's entry in [Diagnostics](/docs/guide/diagnostics). Where the fix is known, your editor offers it as a quick fix, and `wf check --format json` lists it as edits.\n\n**New errors** — each is a program that cannot work as written:\n\n- **Types** (`T`): a field, member or method that does not exist; a value of the wrong type; a value that may be `null` read as if it were not; a `match` that misses a case; arithmetic on what is not a number; a comparison that is always the same; an `await` where it cannot run; a name nothing declares.\n- **Writing what cannot change** (`X01`): a `const`, a `derived` value, a prop, a route parameter, a loop variable.\n- **Components** (`C`): a required prop left out, a prop the component does not declare (`C02`), a part outside its owner.\n- **Routes** (`R`): a link to a path no page matches (`R01`), a `:param` with no page parameter, an `app` with no `Router` or two; two pages on one path (`S04`).\n- **Forms** (`F`): `bind:` to something that cannot be written, or to a state the control cannot hold; a `validate` no control can satisfy.\n- **Reactivity** (`X`): an effect that writes what it reads every run, a `derived` value that calls an action, `use` of a store nothing declares.\n- **Data and translations** (`D`, `I`): a persisted value storage cannot keep; a `t(\"key\")` no translation has, a placeholder the call does not pass, a locale the project does not list.\n- **Literals and styles**: a date that does not exist, money with too many decimals, a key combination no keyboard sends, a `$token` the theme does not declare.\n- **The security policy**: a page's `head \{ script(src: …) \}` from an origin the config does not declare (`E902`).\n\n**Lowering what you cannot fix today.** `\"lints\": \{ \"R01\": \"warn\" \}` in the config lowers a code or a family; an error may be lowered only when it ships no broken page — `R01`, `C02`, `S04`, `I01`, `I02`, `I04`. One finding on one line is accepted with `// wf-allow(CODE)` ([Diagnostics](/docs/guide/diagnostics#u07-an-allow-that-allows-nothing)).\n\n**What the compiler now writes for you** instead of reporting:\n\n- A change inside a state's value — `form.name = v`, `items[i].done = v`, `items.sort()` — is compiled into the state given an updated copy, so what reads it repaints and `persist` keeps it.\n- A value spliced into an address — `to:`, `navigate()`, `fetch()` — is encoded where it stands for a value.\n- A `match` expression that covers every case of an enum needs no `else`.\n\n**Behaviour that changed:**\n\n- **`wf serve` answers a missing fetch with a 404.** A request that is not a page navigation, for a file the build did not write, used to get the single-page shell — a page of HTML handed to `JSON.parse`. It is a `404` now, under `wf verify` and `wf test` too, and `wf serve` prints a line for each. Point the page at a server that has the API, or put a file in `public/`.\n- **An empty path parameter is not sent.** `Backend.user(id: \"\")` with `at \"users/:id\"` used to request `users/`, the whole collection; it now ends as `.aborted`, and a resource over it stays `loading`.\n- **One list item that throws no longer blanks the list**: the rest draws, and under `wf serve` the item says what it threw.\n- **An effect that never settles is stopped** after 100 runs instead of overflowing the stack.\n- **Each build keeps what its persisted values start as** in `.wf-cache/`, so the next can warn about a shape that changed (`D04`) and `wf verify --returning-visitor` can load your pages as a returning reader. Keep `.wf-cache/` between CI runs if you can.\n\nNew: `wf check`, `wf explain`, `--format json|sarif|github` and `--deny-warnings` on `wf build` and `wf check`, quick fixes in the editor, `persist … \{ key: id \}` for one stored value per instance, and `wf verify` visiting `:param` routes. [Command line](/docs/guide/cli) has each.")

        Heading("4.2", id: "4-2").h2

        Markdown("**`external` is gone.** Run `wf migrate`: a remote module becomes a `meta.scripts` entry, a local module a plain script under `src/` with its signatures as JSDoc, and an `external element` call `Element(\"tag\", …)`. A call into a remote module that `external` typed is `Any` afterwards.\n\n**Every `.js` file under `src/` now ships**, linked on every page before the compiled code. `wf migrate` lists the ones it finds; move any that are not meant for the browser out of `src/`.\n\nNew:\n\n- **Your own JavaScript**: a plain `.js` file under `src/`, its top-level functions in scope by name, typed by its JSDoc ([chapter 32](/docs/guide/interop)).\n- **`meta.scripts`** loads a library from a CDN, a module with `as:`, and names its `globals`.\n- **`mount:`, `update:`, `cleanup:` on any element**, and a `wf:render` event for a script that finds elements itself.\n- **`Element(\"tag-name\", …)`** places any custom element.\n- **`class:` takes a map and a list**: `\{ \"is-done\": done \}`, `[\"card\", tone, if open \{ \"is-open\" \}]`; an `if` with no `else` is `null` when it fails ([chapter 15](/docs/guide/styling#classes-and-your-own-stylesheet)).\n- **`V04`** warns about a `class:` that borrows one of the built-ins' `wf-` classes.\n\nFixed: what an `if` branch, a `match` arm, a list item or a slot fill made — a timer, an effect, a `Host`'s cleanup — now goes when its page does; a route change used to leave it running.")

        Heading("4.1", id: "4-1").h2

        Markdown("Nothing to change in your code. New:\n\n- **Offline**: name `offline` in the config for a service worker, writes kept for later, and an update flow ([chapter 19](/docs/guide/offline)).\n- **Peers**: `peer link = rtc(signal: …)`, a WebRTC data channel to another page ([chapter 18](/docs/guide/realtime#a-peer)).\n- **`env` from a `.env` file and the shell**, on top of the config ([chapter 30](/docs/guide/environments)).\n- **Unknown config keys are reported**, with the key they probably meant.\n- **Pagination**: `paginate: .page`, `.items`, `.loadMore()`, `.hasMore`.\n- **`?lang=` opens a page in that locale**, as the `hreflang` alternates promise.\n- **`class`-exported libraries work through `external`**: a class is constructed when called.\n- **Every standard DOM event** is accepted in `on …`.\n\nFixed, and worth knowing because they could have bitten you:\n\n- **A private `env` value no longer reaches the bundle.** 4.0 refused a page that *read* a private name, but still wrote the whole `env` map into `app.js`. If you deployed 4.0 or 4.0.1 with a secret in `env`, rotate it.\n- An action's parameters are its own: `action move(by: Number)` read `by` as a signal and threw.\n- `head \{ \}` tags may read a page's `derived` values.\n- A resource's `state`, `data` and `error`, and a socket's `messages`, show their values in text.\n- `navigator` and `location` compile as the browser's.\n- Two declarations on one line — `theme T \{ color-primary: #0F766E  radius-md: 14px \}` — are two, not one.\n- `S04` reports two pages on one route; `A12` counts an `h1` in each branch of an `if` as one.")

        Heading("4.0", id: "4-0").h2

        Markdown("4.0 changed what the compiler allows. `wf migrate` handles the mechanical part and names every place that needs a decision:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf migrate --check"),
                Line(command: "wf migrate")
            ])

        Markdown("- **`env` is public or private.** A page may only read a name beginning `PUBLIC_` or listed in `public_env`. The migration adds every name your pages already read to `public_env` and prints the list: read it, because public means anyone who opens the site can read the value.\n- **An `on*` attribute is an error** (`onclick: \"…\"`); write `on click \{ \}`.\n- **A URL a browser would run is refused** — `javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, `blob:`, `file:` in `href:`, `src:`, `to:`, `poster:` or `navigate()`.\n- **Stores are built on first read**, not at boot. A store whose set-up must run anyway takes `eager: true`.\n- **A `persist` value follows other tabs.** `sync: false` opts out.\n- **`WF.store` and `WF.host` were renamed** in the runtime surface.\n- **Layouts stay as written**: a `Row` no longer turns into a column on a narrow screen by itself. The migration adds `.stacks` to keep the old behaviour.")

        Heading("3.x", id: "3-x").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent 3 introduced the grammar this guide describes: lowercase declarations, one positional argument, flags after a dot, `on click \{ \}` handlers, `style \{ \}` blocks of CSS.")

        Heading("From WebFluent 2", id: "from-webfluent-2").h2

        Markdown("A WebFluent 2 file (`Page Home (path: \"/\") \{ … \}`) is refused by `wf build` with a pointer to the migration. `wf migrate` rewrites every `.wf` under `src/` into the current grammar — a change of spelling that builds to what it built before — and `wf migrate --wfx` writes the result in the indented layout.")
    }
}
